The barbarian is probably the least interesting class in the game as
it stands right now. It sits squarely in the middle of the original
undifferentiated combat wombat class group and is little more than a
halfway point between the valkyrie and the caveman (and while those
two are more different from each other than they are in vanilla,
they're still pretty similar).

The basic design idea behind most of these ideas is encouraging
barbarians to be fast and reckless, where speed and recklessness on
the part of the character should coincide with a certain amount of
slow careful thought on the part of the player.

STARTING INVENTORY:

EITHER (two handed sword and axe) OR (battle axe and short sword)
2-4 uncursed potions of speed
0-3 uncursed potions of booze
a horn (70% tooled, 10% fire, 10% frost, 10% plenty)

That's it. No food and no armor. The lack of food is an encouragement
to get killing things right away. More on the armor issue follows.

--
AC BONUS:

Barbarians are at their best when rushing boldly into combat without
concern for bodily harm. Armor can only slow them down and suggests an
unflattering meekness.

Barbarians have a base AC of 12 instead of 10, but get a +1 AC bonus
for every armor slot(shirt, body armor, cloak, hat, boots, gloves,
shield) they AREN'T wearing. This means that they start with a naked
AC of 5 but gain little or no additional benefit from most of the
common early armor.

---

RECKLESSNESS BONUS:

These following benefits are only effective while wearing two or fewer
pieces of armor in any of the seven slots. Upon putting on a third
piece of armor, the barbarian gets a "Wearing so much armor makes you
feel less dangerous message".

XP1: Magic Cancellation 1
XP4: Magic Cancellation 2
XP8: Magic Cancellation 3
XP12: Magic Resistance

Also, an additional +1 AC bonus and +1 Damage bonus at every third
level (3, 6, 9, etc.)

This means that barbarians would be in the unique position of being
able to wear SDSM and a Cloak of Displacement while still having both
magic resistance and MC3. Of course, to do so they would have to
eschew speed boots, helm of brilliance, gauntlets of power, etc.

As a quick case study: assuming no divine protection, a level fifteen
barbarian wearing +4 SDSM and a +4 Cloak of Displacement would have an
AC of -16 and a damage bonus of +5. If they put on a full standard
Ascension Kit, they would have in the area of -27 AC in addition to
the other effects of the magic armor, but no Damage bonus and a lot of
additional weight.


---

NEGATIVES:

The monster generation soft clock increases at a significantly faster
rate for barbarians, reaching its invocation level maximum as early as
turn 20,000.

Barbarians tend to encounter difficult monsters somewhat sooner. The
monster generation formula for barbarians is [difficulty <=
((xplvl*1.25) + dlvl)/2] instead of [difficulty <= (xplvl+dlvl)/2]

Rodney related effects happen slighly more often to barbarians after
the invocation.

Engraving the e-word results in a "You can feel $god's disdain at such
cowardice" message and sets your alignment to -1 or $currentalign - 5,
whichever is lower. Barbarians may well prefer to roll a tooled horn
in their starting inventory rather than one of the magic variants.


---

HEART OF AHRIMAN:

That the heart has a luckstone as its base item is suddenly more of a
boon, as barbarians may wish to skip mine's end in order to keep their
turn count down.

When carried, the heart protects its owner, conveying a +3 AC bonus.

When invoked, the heart acts as a scroll of confuse monster with the
same beatitude.

---

OTHER MINOR IDEAS:

Barbarians get a small Damage bonus when confused.

New artifact: Sword of the Phoenix (from Conan) - Chaotic Two Handed
Sword (alignment adjusts for neutral barbarians). +1d8 damage and +1d5
to hit. The Sword of the Phoenix confers warning and conflict when
wielded. This can be obtained as a sacrifice gift (though not as a
first gift, that's still cleaver) or is granted as a crowning gift to
barbarians wielding two handed swords.

YAFM: "You avert your eyes and mourn the loss." when diluting booze or
causing it to clear with an amethyst.

---

Obviously, these ideas would represent a pretty dramatic change.
Almost certainly some of the ideas in here are better than others, and
almost all would need some tweaking to find a balance that works. I
think that in the end, the barbarian would end up somewhat harder than
before. I'm curious to know what everyone else on RGRN thinks of these
ideas and what ideas other people might have about how the Barbarian
could be more interesting.


